WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.448
CI/CD compromise can turn the build pipeline into an attack path.

2
00:00:04.448 --> 00:00:13.756
Build credentials, deployment tokens, source control permissions, artifact repositories, and runner environments all require protection.

3
00:00:13.756 --> 00:00:20.189
Unsigned updates and unverified artifacts make it difficult to know whether code is authentic.

4
00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:26.964
Integrity checks help prevent tampering between development, build, release, and deployment stages.

5
00:00:26.964 --> 00:00:36.271
Insecure deserialization is a data integrity problem because the application trusts serialized input to reconstruct objects or behavior.

6
00:00:36.271 --> 00:00:42.704
Attackers may use that trust to trigger unexpected code paths or manipulate application state.

7
00:00:42.704 --> 00:00:56.254
Pipeline integrity depends on separation of duties, access restrictions, reviewed changes, protected branches, reproducible builds where practical, and traceability from source to deployed artifact.

8
00:00:56.254 --> 00:00:59.060
Verify artifact integrity and provenance.

9
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.729
Sign code and protect release channels.

10
00:01:01.729 --> 00:01:07.067
Secure CI/CD pipelines with least privilege, approvals, and monitored changes.

11
00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:19.522
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SI-7 - Software, Firmware, and Information Integrity; CM-5 - Access Restrictions for Change; SA-10 - Developer Configuration Management.

